What the exception does
-----------------------

The GPL on its own would make any program that links Kerosene a covered
work, so a game built on it would have to be GPL too. The Kerosene Exception
grants an additional permission that the GPL's section 7 allows: you may
link Kerosene, statically or dynamically, into a game and convey the game
under terms of your own. That permission has conditions, and the same
section 7 lets the project attach a few requirements. Read LICENSE-EXCEPTION
for the exact words; in short:

  * A game built on Kerosene is yours. Your code, levels, scripts and assets
    carry whatever terms you like, closed or open. What you owe: the engine
    part of the game stays under the GPL with its source available, the
    game carries a notice that it is built with Kerosene and says where the
    engine source is, and it shows an attribution screen when it starts --
    "Built with Kerosene", before or with its first interactive screen.
    `kiln --ship` writes the licence texts and the notice for you.

  * A modified engine is a modified Kerosene, whatever you call it and
    wherever you put the changed files. If you convey it, alone or inside a
    game, the whole modified engine is under the GPL with the exception; it
    says plainly that it is "modified from Kerosene" or "built from
    Kerosene", names the version or commit it diverged from, and links to
    Kerosene's source -- in its own notice, in the notice of every game built
    on it, and on the attribution screen of every game built on it. A game
    shipped closed on a modified engine additionally publishes the complete
    source of that engine where anyone can get it free of charge, not only
    to the people who receive the game.

  * The Kerosene name is not granted by the licence. A modified engine may
    call itself Kerosene-something ("Kerosene: Ultimate", "Kerosene NG") only
    if it does the above and publishes its whole source, as one engine
    rather than as changed files, under the same terms. Any other name owes
    only what the licence and the exception say.

  * The attribution screen requirement is inherited: a modified engine must
    keep it, so that every game on every descendant of Kerosene still shows
    where it came from.

Whichever of those you are, the project's preference is the same: if you
change Kerosene itself, please contribute the change back as a pull request
instead of releasing a modified Kerosene of your own. Use the updated engine,
and the fix exists once, reviewed and maintained, rather than once per fork.

Contributions
-------------

A patch to Kerosene is offered under the same terms the project uses:
GPL-3.0-or-later WITH the Kerosene Exception. That matters more than it
would for a plain GPL project, because the exception is an additional
permission and a contribution offered under the GPL alone would carry no
such permission, and the file it touched could no longer be linked into a
closed game. There is no CLA; the SPDX line in every file records the terms,
and sending a pull request against this repository is accepting them.

Independent reimplementation
----------------------------

Kerosene is an independent reimplementation. It is not affiliated with,
endorsed by, or sponsored by Valve Corporation or id Software LLC, and it
contains no source code, assets, or data files from the Source engine, the
Source SDK, Hammer, Quake, or any other product of either company.

The project was called VoidEngine until August 2026. It was renamed because
an unrelated engine built on id Tech 6 already uses that name; there is no
relationship between the two, and no code, assets or formats are shared with
it. Kerosene is likewise unaffiliated with Kerosene Games, a game publisher
of that name.

It is not a *clean-room* reimplementation, and does not claim to be: it
was written with knowledge of how id's published compilers work, so the
provenance section below states plainly which modules follow that
structure. Everything here was written from scratch in Rust.

"Valve", "Source", "Source 2", "Hammer", "Half-Life", "Portal", "Steam"
and "Quake" are trademarks of their respective owners. Where those names
appear in Kerosene's documentation and source comments, they are used
nominatively -- to say what a piece of this project is analogous to, or
which published technique it follows -- and no claim of affiliation is
made or implied.

Kerosene defines its own file formats. They are deliberately named and
byte-tagged so they cannot be confused with anyone else's:

    .keromap  .kerobsp  .keroprt  .keroleak
    .kerotex  .keromat  .keromdl  .vault

None of them is compatible with, readable by, or produced from any Valve
or id format. Kerosene cannot open Source or Quake content and does not
attempt to.

